00:03:06.090 --> 00:03:08.820
Guns are more than a bit
player in American history.

00:03:08.820 --> 00:03:10.380
By some historians accounts,

00:03:10.380 --> 00:03:13.320
the American Revolution broke
out over anger at Britain

00:03:13.320 --> 00:03:15.930
for stopping shipments
of firearms to America

00:03:15.930 --> 00:03:19.230
and for confiscating colonists'
limited weapon stockpiles.

00:03:19.230 --> 00:03:21.480
I still think the story
about tea is cute though.

00:03:21.480 --> 00:03:24.000
The Founding Fathers saw
guns as a useful tool

00:03:24.000 --> 00:03:26.640
for hunting and for collective protection.

00:03:26.640 --> 00:03:28.620
Owning a gun was mostly mandatory

00:03:28.620 --> 00:03:30.600
for a certain subsection of white men,

00:03:30.600 --> 00:03:32.940
and some colonies required
them to carry their guns

00:03:32.940 --> 00:03:34.860
to church and public meetings.

00:03:34.860 --> 00:03:37.210
Imagine getting tooled
up and going to Mass.

00:03:37.210 --> 00:03:38.550
Oh, you wouldn't go to Mass

00:03:38.550 --> 00:03:39.840
'cause they hated Catholics then.

00:03:39.840 --> 00:03:42.720
As tensions between the
colonies and Britain heated up,

00:03:42.720 --> 00:03:45.150
guns seemed like the only line of defense

00:03:45.150 --> 00:03:46.830
against an oppressive monarch.

00:03:46.830 --> 00:03:49.260
Gun culture looked very different, though.

00:03:49.260 --> 00:03:50.850
Guns were privately owned,

00:03:50.850 --> 00:03:52.830
but they were seen as collective assets,

00:03:52.830 --> 00:03:56.580
a shared stockpile to be
used by citizen militias.

00:03:56.580 --> 00:03:58.710
And colonial legislation dictated that,

00:03:58.710 --> 00:04:00.120
in case of emergency,

00:04:00.120 --> 00:04:03.142
the government could
briefly repossess your gun

00:04:03.142 --> 00:04:04.650
for use in battle.

00:04:04.650 --> 00:04:07.708
Yes, they could literally
come into your house

00:04:07.708 --> 00:04:09.680
and take your gun.

00:04:09.680 --> 00:04:11.490
(dramatic music)

00:04:11.490 --> 00:04:12.690
Another important thing.

00:04:12.690 --> 00:04:14.970
The Founding Fathers
didn't only love guns,

00:04:14.970 --> 00:04:17.250
they also loved gun control.

00:04:17.250 --> 00:04:19.110
Like how, as a white man,

00:04:19.110 --> 00:04:21.780
you could only own a gun if
you met certain criteria,

00:04:21.780 --> 00:04:23.910
like having a clean criminal record,

00:04:23.910 --> 00:04:25.860
swearing loyalty to the union,

00:04:25.860 --> 00:04:28.590
or in some places, not being Catholic.

00:04:28.590 --> 00:04:30.993
Which if you were to
witness my family reunions,

00:04:30.993 --> 00:04:34.260
would make a lot of sense.

00:04:34.260 --> 00:04:35.850
Too much booze for guns.

00:04:35.850 --> 00:04:37.290
And, of course, Black people

00:04:37.290 --> 00:04:39.360
essentially couldn't own guns at all.

00:04:39.360 --> 00:04:42.810
And even if you did meet all
the criteria to own a gun,

00:04:42.810 --> 00:04:45.180
your use of it was heavily regulated.

00:04:45.180 --> 00:04:46.830
You had to register it
with the government,

00:04:46.830 --> 00:04:48.990
and there were regular
inspections to make sure

00:04:48.990 --> 00:04:51.180
it was ready to be used by the militia.

00:04:51.180 --> 00:04:53.220
Certain places had even stricter laws.

00:04:53.220 --> 00:04:55.890
In Boston, you couldn't
keep a loaded firearm

00:04:55.890 --> 00:04:58.950
inside a building, not even your own home.

00:04:58.950 --> 00:05:00.480
Basically, the Founding Fathers

00:05:00.480 --> 00:05:02.370
seem to have believed
in gun control measures

00:05:02.370 --> 00:05:04.140
that were conducive to public safety.

00:05:04.140 --> 00:05:07.470
And wigs. They also
really believed in wigs.

00:05:07.470 --> 00:05:09.270
I guess they were self-hating bald men

00:05:09.270 --> 00:05:10.650
and thus I don't respect them.

00:05:10.650 --> 00:05:12.810
All of this amounted to an early America

00:05:12.810 --> 00:05:15.540
that wasn't an especially
violent place to be,

00:05:15.540 --> 00:05:16.800
except for the whole war with Britain

00:05:16.800 --> 00:05:19.140
and genocide against
Native American stuff.

00:05:19.140 --> 00:05:21.330
Not a lot of guns were made here.

00:05:21.330 --> 00:05:23.850
And those that were were
handcrafted by gunsmiths

00:05:23.850 --> 00:05:25.740
who, like today's task rabbits

00:05:25.740 --> 00:05:27.750
typically had to work multiple other gigs

00:05:27.750 --> 00:05:30.330
because there wasn't enough
demand for their product.

00:05:30.330 --> 00:05:32.010
Shout out to all my "Real Housewives"

00:05:32.010 --> 00:05:34.560
and lower tier "Bachelor"
contestants out there.

00:05:34.560 --> 00:05:36.360
But in the early 19th century,

00:05:36.360 --> 00:05:38.880
the fledgling U.S. army wanted to beef up,

00:05:38.880 --> 00:05:41.400
specifically by acquiring uniform guns

00:05:41.400 --> 00:05:43.080
with interchangeable parts.

00:05:43.080 --> 00:05:45.630
Previously, every gun had been custom made

00:05:45.630 --> 00:05:47.040
with custom measurements.

00:05:47.040 --> 00:05:49.770
So, industrialists started
inventing the technology

00:05:49.770 --> 00:05:51.960
that would make gunsmiths irrelevant,

00:05:51.960 --> 00:05:55.268
infrastructure for the mass
production of standardized guns.

00:05:55.268 --> 00:05:58.110
Their plan was to score
big government contracts

00:05:58.110 --> 00:05:59.130
and make bank.

00:05:59.130 --> 00:06:01.512
Selling guns commercially to individuals,

00:06:01.512 --> 00:06:03.540
didn't even occur to them.

00:06:03.540 --> 00:06:05.940
One key player in this was Samuel Colt,

00:06:05.940 --> 00:06:08.580
also known as Dr. Samuel Colt,

00:06:08.580 --> 00:06:10.740
a performer known for staging big shows

00:06:10.740 --> 00:06:12.519
where he gave people
too much laughing gas.

00:06:12.519 --> 00:06:13.352
(audience laughing)

00:06:13.352 --> 00:06:15.150
But this guy saw more potential

00:06:15.150 --> 00:06:17.070
in the mass manufacturing of guns.

00:06:17.070 --> 00:06:20.220
And he went all in, inventing
the revolving cylinder,

00:06:20.220 --> 00:06:22.860
which allowed guns to hold
multiple rounds of ammunition,

00:06:22.860 --> 00:06:25.200
rendering them significantly more lethal

00:06:25.200 --> 00:06:28.255
and definitely not as fun as laughing gas.

00:06:28.255 --> 00:06:30.660
Gun makers would continue to come up with

00:06:30.660 --> 00:06:32.880
other ways to make their guns more lethal

00:06:32.880 --> 00:06:34.440
as a selling point.

00:06:34.440 --> 00:06:38.400
But Colt quickly realized he
had two really big problems:

00:06:38.400 --> 00:06:41.490
One, the army didn't want
to buy large quantities

00:06:41.490 --> 00:06:42.960
of these newfangled guns.

00:06:42.960 --> 00:06:45.180
And two, operating at capacity,

00:06:45.180 --> 00:06:49.080
his factory could create far
more guns than he could sell.

00:06:49.080 --> 00:06:51.540
There just wasn't enough
demand for something that,

00:06:51.540 --> 00:06:55.110
at the time, was largely
seen as an instrument of war.

00:06:55.110 --> 00:06:56.990
So Colt and his fellow gun manufacturers

00:06:56.990 --> 00:06:58.710
had to get creative;

00:06:58.710 --> 00:07:00.780
they had to artificially manufacture

00:07:00.780 --> 00:07:03.780
a civilian demand for their product.

00:07:03.780 --> 00:07:08.780
What a thing to artificially
invent demand for a product.

00:07:09.030 --> 00:07:10.053
That's crazy.

00:07:11.160 --> 00:07:12.480
I'm glad they stopped doing that.

00:07:12.480 --> 00:07:14.070
I'm glad that's never happened since,

00:07:14.070 --> 00:07:15.990
that would suck to live in that world.

00:07:15.990 --> 00:07:19.140
One company hilariously
patented a cane gun,

00:07:19.140 --> 00:07:21.690
which, yes, is a cane that shoots bullets.

00:07:21.690 --> 00:07:23.721
Tragically, it wasn't a big hit.

00:07:23.721 --> 00:07:24.570
(dramatic percussion music)

00:07:24.570 --> 00:07:27.240
For his part, Colt was all about marketing

00:07:27.240 --> 00:07:28.650
and heavy discounts.

00:07:28.650 --> 00:07:31.590
But, frustrated by
lackluster American demand,

00:07:31.590 --> 00:07:33.690
he started thinking internationally.

00:07:33.690 --> 00:07:37.320
A war-happy Europe was all
too willing to fill the void.

00:07:37.320 --> 00:07:39.127
As historian Pamela Haag puts it,

00:07:39.127 --> 00:07:42.097
"It was the European
regimes of the mid-1800s,

00:07:42.097 --> 00:07:43.207
"embattled with each other

00:07:43.207 --> 00:07:45.097
"and brimming with imperial ambition,

00:07:45.097 --> 00:07:47.700
"where the demand for guns ran highest."

00:07:47.700 --> 00:07:50.400
Colt began to see the world as his market.

00:07:50.400 --> 00:07:52.530
Arming Europe would
embolden those countries

00:07:52.530 --> 00:07:55.714
to encroach on Asia, and then, yay,

00:07:55.714 --> 00:07:58.170
Asia would need guns, too,

00:07:58.170 --> 00:08:03.170
which is very cool and not at
all cynical logic, Mr. Colt.

00:08:03.210 --> 00:08:04.410
Dr. Colt.

00:08:04.410 --> 00:08:06.750
He traveled abroad bringing lavish gifts

00:08:06.750 --> 00:08:08.220
to important statesmen,

00:08:08.220 --> 00:08:10.830
and arming anyone who could sign a check.

00:08:10.830 --> 00:08:14.550
That included supplying
weapons to Russia and Turkey

00:08:14.550 --> 00:08:16.500
in their ongoing war.

00:08:16.500 --> 00:08:18.300
It's fun when people killing each other

00:08:18.300 --> 00:08:19.470
is good for business.

00:08:19.470 --> 00:08:23.460
By the 1880s, nearly all guns
in Europe were made in America

00:08:23.460 --> 00:08:25.140
via what was functionally the beginning

00:08:25.140 --> 00:08:26.730
of modern globalization.

00:08:26.730 --> 00:08:29.220
But back to the 1850s for a sec.

00:08:29.220 --> 00:08:31.020
Gun makers still faced a problem.

00:08:31.020 --> 00:08:33.960
Gun sales were better during times of war.

00:08:33.960 --> 00:08:36.750
But relying on government
contracts meant poor sales

00:08:36.750 --> 00:08:37.980
during times of peace.

00:08:37.980 --> 00:08:40.860
One of Colt's competitors,
Oliver Winchester had an idea.

00:08:40.860 --> 00:08:42.780
What if instead of wedding himself

00:08:42.780 --> 00:08:44.790
to American government contracts,

00:08:44.790 --> 00:08:47.310
he cultivated a commercial gun market.

00:08:47.310 --> 00:08:51.270
Winchester imagined his ideal
customers not to be soldiers,

00:08:51.270 --> 00:08:54.330
but to be what Haag
calls citizen-defenders

00:08:54.330 --> 00:08:57.780
who would buy guns in defense
of their homes and land.

00:08:57.780 --> 00:08:59.520
This is when gun ownership started

00:08:59.520 --> 00:09:02.580
being less about the early
logic of collective defense

00:09:02.580 --> 00:09:04.020
against an oppressive government,

00:09:04.020 --> 00:09:06.090
and more about individual self-defense

00:09:06.090 --> 00:09:08.190
against a dangerous frontier.

00:09:08.190 --> 00:09:11.220
The struggle to cultivate this
dream market was, for a time,

00:09:11.220 --> 00:09:12.900
abated by the Civil War,

00:09:12.900 --> 00:09:14.520
which, as you might imagine,

00:09:14.520 --> 00:09:17.187
created a huge demand for guns.

00:09:17.187 --> 00:09:20.760
And for a man like Colt, it
offered two hungry markets:

00:09:20.760 --> 00:09:22.500
the union and the confederacy.

00:09:22.500 --> 00:09:24.600
That's right, this ambitious dickbag

00:09:24.600 --> 00:09:27.150
armed both sides of the conflict,

00:09:27.150 --> 00:09:29.970
causing the New York Times
to accuse him of treason.

00:09:29.970 --> 00:09:32.340
But the wars end brought new problems.

00:09:32.340 --> 00:09:35.490
Hundreds of thousands of
firearms flooded the markets.

00:09:35.490 --> 00:09:38.040
So many former soldiers now owned guns

00:09:38.040 --> 00:09:40.260
that the gun market turned abysmal

00:09:40.260 --> 00:09:43.650
and gun makers looked back
to international customers.

00:09:43.650 --> 00:09:45.960
And they were not discerning.

00:09:45.960 --> 00:09:48.997
As scholars Rachel Stohl,
and Suzette Grillot write,

00:09:48.997 --> 00:09:52.327
"Manufacturers took advantage
of laissez-faire economics

00:09:52.327 --> 00:09:54.187
"and sold weapons to interested customers,

00:09:54.187 --> 00:09:57.484
"no matter who or where
they might have been,"

00:09:57.484 --> 00:10:00.480
arming despots and dictators alike.

00:10:00.480 --> 00:10:03.630
And presumably finally, the Catholics.

00:10:03.630 --> 00:10:06.300
American guns spread everywhere from Fiji,

00:10:06.300 --> 00:10:09.240
where they replaced boomerangs, to Alaska,

00:10:09.240 --> 00:10:11.820
where they armed Inuit people for hunting.

00:10:11.820 --> 00:10:13.950
At the time in say, Britain,

00:10:13.950 --> 00:10:16.740
gun dealers scrutinize
each customer's character

00:10:16.740 --> 00:10:17.640
and integrity.

00:10:17.640 --> 00:10:19.537
In America, the vibe was more,

00:10:19.537 --> 00:10:20.557
"You get a gun.

00:10:20.557 --> 00:10:22.507
"He gets a gun, she gets a gun.

00:10:22.507 --> 00:10:23.647
"The dog gets a gun.

00:10:23.647 --> 00:10:26.250
"Take this gun for 50% off guns.

00:10:26.250 --> 00:10:29.670
So we know by now that
civilians owning guns

00:10:29.670 --> 00:10:32.610
is not as American as butter Jesus.

00:10:32.610 --> 00:10:37.410
So how did America go from
gun-agnostic to gun-loving?

00:10:37.410 --> 00:10:39.990
Advertising. And lots of it.

00:10:39.990 --> 00:10:42.150
Marketing historian Robert Henning argues

00:10:42.150 --> 00:10:44.850
that advertisements were key to inventing

00:10:44.850 --> 00:10:47.070
and shaping American gun culture.

00:10:47.070 --> 00:10:50.070
This advertising relied
on the romanticization

00:10:50.070 --> 00:10:51.390
of the Wild West,

00:10:51.390 --> 00:10:53.100
where the well-armed frontiersman

00:10:53.100 --> 00:10:57.150
was literally manifesting
manifest destiny.

00:10:57.150 --> 00:10:59.580
Or just literally manifesting destiny.

00:10:59.580 --> 00:11:00.413
I don't know.

00:11:00.413 --> 00:11:01.980
As legal professor Adam Winkler

00:11:01.980 --> 00:11:03.787
notes in his book "Gunfight,"

00:11:03.787 --> 00:11:06.307
"Cultural observers have
argued that the frontier

00:11:06.307 --> 00:11:09.337
"was not only the root of
the American character,

00:11:09.337 --> 00:11:12.600
"but also the origin of America's
propensity for violence."

00:11:12.600 --> 00:11:15.480
The Wild West-ification of America

00:11:15.480 --> 00:11:17.430
was envisioned through promotional imagery

00:11:17.430 --> 00:11:19.717
of Western frontiersmen,
and taglines like:

00:11:19.717 --> 00:11:20.917
"God created men.

00:11:20.917 --> 00:11:23.010
"Colonel Colt made them equal."

00:11:23.010 --> 00:11:25.620
Okay, so now he's Mr. Colt,

00:11:25.620 --> 00:11:28.560
Dr. Colt and Colonel Colt.

00:11:28.560 --> 00:11:29.393
I don't buy it.

00:11:29.393 --> 00:11:31.890
Meanwhile, wildly
popular dime store novels

00:11:31.890 --> 00:11:34.440
written on the civilized East Coast

00:11:34.440 --> 00:11:37.380
told violent tales of great
gunslingers in the west,

00:11:37.380 --> 00:11:39.330
cementing the frontier's legacy.

00:11:39.330 --> 00:11:41.880
In this way, guns were
presented in popular media

00:11:41.880 --> 00:11:45.660
as the antithesis of proper
East Coast industrial society,

00:11:45.660 --> 00:11:49.050
despite the fact that guns
were solely being manufactured

00:11:49.050 --> 00:11:50.940
in places like New Haven, Connecticut.

00:11:50.940 --> 00:11:54.090
And so the east, mythologizing the west,

00:11:54.090 --> 00:11:56.340
began to really arm the country.

00:11:56.340 --> 00:11:59.640
Winchester also recognized
the importance of imbuing guns

00:11:59.640 --> 00:12:02.520
with a sort of symbolic importance,

00:12:02.520 --> 00:12:05.197
anthropomorphizing their
supposed character.

00:12:05.197 --> 00:12:07.927
"The great advantage of
this rifle over all others

00:12:07.927 --> 00:12:10.980
"consists in the moral
effect it has," he wrote.

00:12:10.980 --> 00:12:12.863
I've never read a book of moral philosophy

00:12:12.863 --> 00:12:14.797
where someone's like,

00:12:14.797 --> 00:12:17.820
"Chapter two, why guns are virtuous?"

00:12:17.820 --> 00:12:20.790
Guns were starting to become
a sign of self-sovereignty,

00:12:20.790 --> 00:12:23.550
a symbol of American individualism.

00:12:23.550 --> 00:12:25.170
So, you might be thinking:

00:12:25.170 --> 00:12:27.720
America's idea of violence
came out of the west.

00:12:27.720 --> 00:12:31.230
So the west must have
been super violent, right?

00:12:31.230 --> 00:12:32.820
Not so fast, cowboy.

00:12:32.820 --> 00:12:35.160
The Wild West had tons of guns,

00:12:35.160 --> 00:12:36.660
but it also had something in common

00:12:36.660 --> 00:12:37.830
with the Founding Fathers,

00:12:37.830 --> 00:12:39.030
lots of gun control.

00:12:39.030 --> 00:12:41.730
You may picture the Wild
West as one big rowdy,

00:12:41.730 --> 00:12:43.110
gun-slinging saloon,

00:12:43.110 --> 00:12:45.600
but evidence shows that
murder rates were minimal.

00:12:45.600 --> 00:12:49.560
Most people felt so safe they
didn't even lock their doors.

00:12:49.560 --> 00:12:50.797
As Winkler notes,

00:12:50.797 --> 00:12:54.367
"Almost everyone carried firearms
in the untamed wilderness,

00:12:54.367 --> 00:12:57.997
"which was full of dangerous
Natives, outlaws, and bears.

00:12:57.997 --> 00:12:59.347
"In the frontier towns, however,

00:12:59.347 --> 00:13:01.477
"where people lived and
businesses operated,

00:13:01.477 --> 00:13:03.307
"the law often forbade people

00:13:03.307 --> 00:13:05.130
"from toting their guns around."

00:13:05.130 --> 00:13:06.840
Some of this was strategic.

00:13:06.840 --> 00:13:09.180
Frontier towns were
small but wanted to grow,

00:13:09.180 --> 00:13:11.670
and to do that, they needed to seem safe.

00:13:11.670 --> 00:13:14.820
While lore of western
gunfights mainstreamed guns

00:13:14.820 --> 00:13:18.480
and cowboys as twin symbols
of an individualistic America,

00:13:18.480 --> 00:13:21.330
the actual west knew
that they couldn't afford

00:13:21.330 --> 00:13:23.190
to be as violent as their mythology.

00:13:23.190 --> 00:13:25.410
Still, the romanticization of the west

00:13:25.410 --> 00:13:27.960
was a highly successful
marketing strategy,

00:13:27.960 --> 00:13:29.580
and around the turn of the century,

00:13:29.580 --> 00:13:31.500
gun sales were booming.

00:13:31.500 --> 00:13:33.720
This is when the gun
really went from serving

00:13:33.720 --> 00:13:36.870
as a tool of necessity to
serving a psychological need.

00:13:36.870 --> 00:13:37.747
As Haag puts it,

00:13:37.747 --> 00:13:40.770
"What was once needed,
now had to be loved."

00:13:40.770 --> 00:13:42.360
Which would be a really beautiful quote

00:13:42.360 --> 00:13:44.670
if it wasn't describing a gun.

00:13:44.670 --> 00:13:47.250
Luckily, it was the perfect time for that.

00:13:47.250 --> 00:13:49.830
Historian Kristin Hoganson, among others,

00:13:49.830 --> 00:13:52.590
has argued that gender-based
anxieties of the time

00:13:52.590 --> 00:13:56.100
engineered a new preoccupation
with a return to manliness

00:13:56.100 --> 00:14:01.100
via boxing, football,
war, and of course, guns.

00:14:01.320 --> 00:14:02.610
For President Teddy Roosevelt,

00:14:02.610 --> 00:14:05.130
the gun was a remedy
against the rapid changes

00:14:05.130 --> 00:14:07.680
of modern life, which had
brought about a corporate economy

00:14:07.680 --> 00:14:11.160
antithetical to the
individualist American mythos.

00:14:11.160 --> 00:14:14.070
Anyway, the mystique of
the gun obscured the fact

00:14:14.070 --> 00:14:16.620
that the gun industry was as much a part

00:14:16.620 --> 00:14:19.230
of the modern corporate
economy as any other.

00:14:19.230 --> 00:14:20.460
When World War I hit,

00:14:20.460 --> 00:14:21.990
the gun industry had to step up,

00:14:21.990 --> 00:14:23.670
with Winchester securing contracts

00:14:23.670 --> 00:14:25.530
to immediately produce the equivalent

00:14:25.530 --> 00:14:27.720
of five years worth of orders.

00:14:27.720 --> 00:14:29.070
Ironically, this time,

00:14:29.070 --> 00:14:32.340
war was a risky
proposition for gun makers.

00:14:32.340 --> 00:14:34.350
They had to take out massive loans

00:14:34.350 --> 00:14:36.900
to expand their factories to
accommodate all the demand,

00:14:36.900 --> 00:14:39.750
and after the war, many
were drowning in debt.

00:14:39.750 --> 00:14:43.260
They tried to rebuild via
massive advertising campaigns

00:14:43.260 --> 00:14:45.390
that continued into the mid-1900s.

00:14:45.390 --> 00:14:47.010
They targeted, among other groups,

00:14:47.010 --> 00:14:49.740
women and boys as young as 12

00:14:49.740 --> 00:14:51.487
via pamphlets that raved about,

00:14:51.487 --> 00:14:54.870
"Four American boys who
are famous rifle shots."

00:14:54.870 --> 00:14:56.610
Which is a real advertising slogan

00:14:56.610 --> 00:14:58.440
and not something written by a spambot.

00:14:58.440 --> 00:15:00.157
Winchester retailers were told to,

00:15:00.157 --> 00:15:01.627
"Put a Winchester into the hands

00:15:01.627 --> 00:15:03.720
"of every youth in your town,"

00:15:03.720 --> 00:15:06.480
with the overall goal of
arming millions of boys

00:15:06.480 --> 00:15:10.470
aged 10 to 16, which is
objectively a pretty wild thing

00:15:10.470 --> 00:15:11.760
to put on your vision board.

00:15:11.760 --> 00:15:13.260
It's crazy that now we're worried about

00:15:13.260 --> 00:15:14.850
young people playing video games,

00:15:14.850 --> 00:15:17.010
but back then we just gave them guns.

00:15:17.010 --> 00:15:20.610
Advertisements stoked fears
that the war had come home,

00:15:20.610 --> 00:15:23.100
and danger lurked on every street.

00:15:23.100 --> 00:15:24.487
Colt's ads warned that,

00:15:24.487 --> 00:15:27.457
"Responsibility for the
protection of your property,

00:15:27.457 --> 00:15:30.487
"your home, your loved ones
is too serious a matter

00:15:30.487 --> 00:15:33.780
"to be entrusted to an
arm of doubtful lineage."

00:15:33.780 --> 00:15:36.390
Guns were touted as totems of masculinity,

00:15:36.390 --> 00:15:39.000
protecting men from becoming soft.

00:15:39.000 --> 00:15:41.850
Images of wholesome young
red-blooded American men

00:15:41.850 --> 00:15:44.520
could assure anyone worried
that America was not,

00:15:44.520 --> 00:15:46.747
in fact, "Degenerating into a nation

00:15:46.747 --> 00:15:48.900
"of lounge lizard anemics,"

00:15:48.900 --> 00:15:51.240
as the Industrial Manufacturer news bureau

00:15:51.240 --> 00:15:52.380
delightfully put it.

00:15:52.380 --> 00:15:54.960
Also if Lounge Lizard
Anemics isn't a band yet,

00:15:54.960 --> 00:15:57.570
someone watching this, start that band.

00:15:57.570 --> 00:15:58.710
You know, you'll have good merch.

00:15:58.710 --> 00:16:01.560
Advertisements, some modeled
after liquor companies,

00:16:01.560 --> 00:16:04.500
were teaching people to
truly love their guns.

00:16:04.500 --> 00:16:06.660
Gradually, this sentiment
helped create consumers

00:16:06.660 --> 00:16:09.510
who saw guns not as objects of necessity,

00:16:09.510 --> 00:16:12.150
but as objects of desire.

00:16:12.150 --> 00:16:14.760
And like me buying merch
outside of a Dead show,

00:16:14.760 --> 00:16:16.530
these kinds of consumers would buy

00:16:16.530 --> 00:16:18.570
way more (beeps) than they needed.

00:16:18.570 --> 00:16:21.600
The lore of American gun
culture was persuasive.

00:16:21.600 --> 00:16:25.117
By 1959, Winchester could
straight-facedly boast the slogan:

00:16:25.117 --> 00:16:28.890
"Winchester: More than a
gun. An American legend."

00:16:28.890 --> 00:16:30.990
A legend they had authored themselves,

00:16:30.990 --> 00:16:33.600
but okay, sure.

00:16:33.600 --> 00:16:35.947
As Haag puts it, "One
answer to the question,

00:16:35.947 --> 00:16:37.657
"why do Americans love guns?"

00:16:37.657 --> 00:16:40.117
"is, simply, that we were invited to do so

00:16:40.117 --> 00:16:41.797
"by those who made and sold them

00:16:41.797 --> 00:16:43.717
"at the moment when
their products had shed

00:16:43.717 --> 00:16:46.830
"much of their more
practical, utilitarian value."

00:16:46.830 --> 00:16:50.100
Still, popular media ran
with Winchester's narrative

00:16:50.100 --> 00:16:51.757
offering that, "casual assertions...

00:16:51.757 --> 00:16:54.007
"that Americans had always loved guns,

00:16:54.007 --> 00:16:56.790
"or that they had a timeless
tradition of gun fluency,"

00:16:56.790 --> 00:16:57.720
as Haag writes.

00:16:57.720 --> 00:16:59.130
For much of our history,

00:16:59.130 --> 00:17:00.720
Americans were actually pretty bad

00:17:00.720 --> 00:17:03.270
at actually shooting the guns.

00:17:03.270 --> 00:17:06.480
In fact, the NRA was
formed after the Civil War,

00:17:06.480 --> 00:17:08.940
specifically because some
veterans were alarmed

00:17:08.940 --> 00:17:12.330
at the horrible marksmanship
of Union soldiers.

00:17:12.330 --> 00:17:13.200
For much of its history,

00:17:13.200 --> 00:17:16.380
the NRA was more about
target-shooting competitions

00:17:16.380 --> 00:17:18.060
than influencing politics.

00:17:18.060 --> 00:17:21.030
Though today its rhetoric
is fiercely anti-government,

00:17:21.030 --> 00:17:23.850
the early NRA relied on major subsidies

00:17:23.850 --> 00:17:25.117
to help build gun ranges

00:17:25.117 --> 00:17:27.420
and taxpayers even got the privilege

00:17:27.420 --> 00:17:30.240
of funding their annual
competition in Ohio.

00:17:30.240 --> 00:17:33.480
But then came the 1920s
and '30s, and with it,

00:17:33.480 --> 00:17:36.720
prohibition and a huge
spike in urban crime.

00:17:36.720 --> 00:17:38.040
And again, with prohibition,

00:17:38.040 --> 00:17:41.130
like why is everyone always
trying to keep the Catholics

00:17:41.130 --> 00:17:42.210
from doing what they love?

00:17:42.210 --> 00:17:45.570
This violence was personified
by the ruthless mobster,

00:17:45.570 --> 00:17:47.460
who was typically armed with a Tommy gun,

00:17:47.460 --> 00:17:50.430
a highly lethal machine
gun that caused violence

00:17:50.430 --> 00:17:52.530
on a scale once unimaginable.

00:17:52.530 --> 00:17:55.320
Gun control remained a tough
political sell, though.

00:17:55.320 --> 00:17:58.110
By now, gun manufacturers
and America's War Department

00:17:58.110 --> 00:18:00.120
had a symbiotic relationship.

00:18:00.120 --> 00:18:01.650
The War Department feared that,

00:18:01.650 --> 00:18:03.900
if gun businesses couldn't find markets

00:18:03.900 --> 00:18:05.310
during times of peace,

00:18:05.310 --> 00:18:08.130
they wouldn't be prepared
for times of war.

00:18:08.130 --> 00:18:10.080
It's hard to imagine
now, but during this era,

00:18:10.080 --> 00:18:12.870
the NRA actually stepped
in and lobbied states

00:18:12.870 --> 00:18:15.060
to enact gun control measures.

00:18:15.060 --> 00:18:17.430
The sheer magnitude of mob violence

00:18:17.430 --> 00:18:19.290
created enough political will in Congress

00:18:19.290 --> 00:18:21.210
to pass the National Firearms Act.

00:18:21.210 --> 00:18:23.460
The law, and subsequent additions to it,

00:18:23.460 --> 00:18:25.260
effectively halted the proliferation

00:18:25.260 --> 00:18:26.880
of machine guns permanently.

00:18:26.880 --> 00:18:29.640
Retrospectively,
historians view it as proof

00:18:29.640 --> 00:18:33.450
that successful gun control
is possible in America.

00:18:33.450 --> 00:18:37.260
And then, things were basically
chill on the gun front.

00:18:37.260 --> 00:18:39.390
Until this happened.

00:18:39.390 --> 00:18:41.340
You may have heard of the Black Panthers,

00:18:41.340 --> 00:18:44.010
an activist group that
organized against racism

00:18:44.010 --> 00:18:46.470
and police brutality, and
believed that arming Black people

00:18:46.470 --> 00:18:49.260
was the best path towards racial justice.

00:18:49.260 --> 00:18:51.630
Though they're maybe best
known for carrying guns

00:18:51.630 --> 00:18:53.580
and having really good
monochromatic outfits,

00:18:53.580 --> 00:18:56.580
they most importantly
provided community resources,

00:18:56.580 --> 00:18:58.950
like their free breakfast
program for children.

00:18:58.950 --> 00:19:00.330
Leader Huey Newton argued

00:19:00.330 --> 00:19:02.520
that the civility of the
Civil Rights Movement

00:19:02.520 --> 00:19:03.780
hadn't fixed anything,

00:19:03.780 --> 00:19:06.120
and more radical action was necessary.

00:19:06.120 --> 00:19:09.030
At one point, this took the
form of 30 armed Black Panthers

00:19:09.030 --> 00:19:11.190
legally wielding guns as they protested

00:19:11.190 --> 00:19:13.050
at the California State House.

00:19:13.050 --> 00:19:15.000
Now's a good moment to talk about the link

00:19:15.000 --> 00:19:17.640
between racism and gun culture.

00:19:17.640 --> 00:19:19.710
Gun regulation has been used for centuries

00:19:19.710 --> 00:19:21.800
to block Black people's access to guns.

00:19:21.800 --> 00:19:23.910
In colonial times, enslaved people

00:19:23.910 --> 00:19:26.940
and even free blacks
weren't allowed to own guns,

00:19:26.940 --> 00:19:30.330
because colonists feared
they'd use them to rise up.

00:19:30.330 --> 00:19:34.410
Hell, the KKK began as a
gun control organization.

00:19:34.410 --> 00:19:35.243
No. Seriously.

00:19:35.243 --> 00:19:36.900
It formed after the Civil War

00:19:36.900 --> 00:19:40.560
to confiscate guns from Black
men, including veterans.

00:19:40.560 --> 00:19:42.930
Guns became an important
means of protection

00:19:42.930 --> 00:19:45.840
for freed Southern Black
people to fight violent racism,

00:19:45.840 --> 00:19:49.110
with some Black men forming
militias to stop lynchings.

00:19:49.110 --> 00:19:52.170
A century later, the Black
Panthers similarly decided

00:19:52.170 --> 00:19:53.880
that carrying guns for self-defense

00:19:53.880 --> 00:19:57.180
was the only way they could
gain respect and safety.

00:19:57.180 --> 00:20:00.480
And that really freaked white people out.

00:20:00.480 --> 00:20:02.820
In fact, the Panthers
so alarmed lawmakers,

00:20:02.820 --> 00:20:03.990
that they helped inspire

00:20:03.990 --> 00:20:08.040
the strictest national gun
regulation America had ever seen:

00:20:08.040 --> 00:20:10.470
the Gun Control Act of 1968,

00:20:10.470 --> 00:20:13.230
along with the Omnibus Crime
Control and Safe Streets Act.

00:20:13.230 --> 00:20:16.380
These laws were also inspired
in part by collective grief

00:20:16.380 --> 00:20:18.750
over a series of political assassinations,

00:20:18.750 --> 00:20:22.020
including MLK, JFK, and Robert Kennedy.

00:20:22.020 --> 00:20:24.660
The laws expanded federal
licensing for guns,

00:20:24.660 --> 00:20:26.310
and they forbade ex-felons,

00:20:26.310 --> 00:20:28.020
people with certain mental illnesses,

00:20:28.020 --> 00:20:30.960
illegal drug users, and
children from owning guns.

00:20:30.960 --> 00:20:32.250
And it restricted imports

00:20:32.250 --> 00:20:34.560
of so-called Saturday Night Special guns,

00:20:34.560 --> 00:20:36.300
which were frequently used in crime

00:20:36.300 --> 00:20:38.730
and different from
"Saturday Night Fever" guns,

00:20:38.730 --> 00:20:39.990
which were real sparkly

00:20:39.990 --> 00:20:42.060
and played disco music when you shot them.

00:20:42.060 --> 00:20:44.640
Note that the NRA supported
some of these measures

00:20:44.640 --> 00:20:46.860
and even reluctantly endorsed the law.

00:20:46.860 --> 00:20:50.310
And yet, even as political
will favored gun control,

00:20:50.310 --> 00:20:53.250
the gun industry still received
some special protection

00:20:53.250 --> 00:20:54.210
from higher-ups.

00:20:54.210 --> 00:20:57.720
It was the sole industry
to be exempt from oversight

00:20:57.720 --> 00:20:59.910
via the new Consumer
Product Safety Commission

00:20:59.910 --> 00:21:02.070
created in 1972.

00:21:02.070 --> 00:21:03.570
According to The Trace,

00:21:03.570 --> 00:21:05.610
this is because house
representatives feared

00:21:05.610 --> 00:21:08.580
a slippery slope that
would lead the agency

00:21:08.580 --> 00:21:10.350
to confiscate people's guns.

00:21:10.350 --> 00:21:14.160
This functionally means that
there's more federal oversight

00:21:14.160 --> 00:21:17.370
over a toy gun than a real one,

00:21:17.370 --> 00:21:19.080
which is too depressing to be funny.

00:21:19.080 --> 00:21:21.840
Seriously, it means that the
government has more oversight

00:21:21.840 --> 00:21:25.380
over this plastic piece of crap than a gun

00:21:25.380 --> 00:21:26.820
that shoots bullets.

00:21:26.820 --> 00:21:28.470
I don't know. Oh, (beeps)!

00:21:28.470 --> 00:21:31.020
It also has real consequences.

00:21:31.020 --> 00:21:33.330
Plenty have pointed out
that gun manufacturers

00:21:33.330 --> 00:21:34.830
could make guns safer,

00:21:34.830 --> 00:21:37.950
by fixing flaws in trigger
locks or personalizing guns

00:21:37.950 --> 00:21:40.230
so they can only be used by their owner.

00:21:40.230 --> 00:21:41.970
But without federal oversight,

00:21:41.970 --> 00:21:44.430
there's no incentive for
the industry to make sure

00:21:44.430 --> 00:21:47.190
all guns sold meet
minimum safety standards.

00:21:47.190 --> 00:21:49.290
But back to 1968.

00:21:49.290 --> 00:21:51.870
The gun control measures that
did make it through Congress

00:21:51.870 --> 00:21:54.000
would have a complicated legacy.

00:21:54.000 --> 00:21:55.057
As Winkler notes,

00:21:55.057 --> 00:21:57.067
"If it hadn't been for
the Black Panthers...

00:21:57.067 --> 00:22:00.150
"there might never have been
a modern gun rights movement."

00:22:00.150 --> 00:22:02.670
That's because, he explains,
these new strict laws,

00:22:02.670 --> 00:22:04.657
which were, "aimed largely at disarming

00:22:04.657 --> 00:22:06.367
"urban black leftist radicals,

00:22:06.367 --> 00:22:09.870
"led to a backlash by
rural white conservatives."

00:22:09.870 --> 00:22:10.950
Now, you may be wondering

00:22:10.950 --> 00:22:13.920
why we haven't mentioned one
of the essential talking points

00:22:13.920 --> 00:22:15.690
of the gun rights debate in America.

00:22:15.690 --> 00:22:17.527
The second amendment, which reads:

00:22:17.527 --> 00:22:19.357
"A well regulated militia,

00:22:19.357 --> 00:22:21.937
"being necessary to for the
security of a free state,

00:22:21.937 --> 00:22:24.217
"the right of the people
to keep and bear arms,

00:22:24.217 --> 00:22:26.100
"shall not be infringed."

00:22:26.100 --> 00:22:27.870
There's a good reason it
hasn't come up, though.

00:22:27.870 --> 00:22:29.910
Until the 1960s, the second amendment

00:22:29.910 --> 00:22:31.920
wasn't one of the popular kids.

00:22:31.920 --> 00:22:33.780
It didn't play into the gun debate,

00:22:33.780 --> 00:22:36.780
and was generally ignored
by legal scholars.

00:22:36.780 --> 00:22:39.120
The Supreme Court did use
it to uphold challenges

00:22:39.120 --> 00:22:40.860
to the 1934 law,

00:22:40.860 --> 00:22:43.500
arguing that sawed-off
shotguns and machine guns

00:22:43.500 --> 00:22:45.420
wouldn't be used by a state militia,

00:22:45.420 --> 00:22:47.100
and thus could be regulated.

00:22:47.100 --> 00:22:49.960
This was the militia
interpretation of the amendment;

00:22:49.960 --> 00:22:53.100
that it only protected gun rights insofar

00:22:53.100 --> 00:22:55.950
as they would apply to
arming a citizen militia.

00:22:55.950 --> 00:22:58.410
But scholars in the '60s
and '70s started arguing

00:22:58.410 --> 00:23:00.720
for a more individual interpretation

00:23:00.720 --> 00:23:02.040
of the second amendment.

00:23:02.040 --> 00:23:05.460
And the NRA went HAM
on this interpretation,

00:23:05.460 --> 00:23:08.010
even bolting an excerpt of the amendment

00:23:08.010 --> 00:23:10.110
to the outside of their headquarters.

00:23:10.110 --> 00:23:12.360
Eventually, the individual interpretation

00:23:12.360 --> 00:23:14.490
would be borne out in a
Supreme Court decision

00:23:14.490 --> 00:23:17.700
on District of Columbia
vs. Heller in 2008,

00:23:17.700 --> 00:23:20.310
which overturned D.C.'s ban on handguns

00:23:20.310 --> 00:23:22.500
in a hotly controversial decision.

00:23:22.500 --> 00:23:26.400
The question of the individual
vs. militia interpretation

00:23:26.400 --> 00:23:28.140
remains contested by scholars.

00:23:28.140 --> 00:23:29.970
For his part, back in 1991,

00:23:29.970 --> 00:23:31.680
former Chief Justice Warren Burger

00:23:31.680 --> 00:23:33.540
called the individual interpretation

00:23:33.540 --> 00:23:34.500
of the second amendment-

00:23:34.500 --> 00:23:37.230
- One of the greatest pieces of fraud,

00:23:37.230 --> 00:23:39.879
I repeat the word fraud
on the American public

00:23:39.879 --> 00:23:42.202
by special interest groups

00:23:42.202 --> 00:23:44.460
that I have ever seen in my lifetime.

00:23:44.460 --> 00:23:46.650
- We're not gonna go too
deep on the second amendment,

00:23:46.650 --> 00:23:49.590
and not just because the
debate is pretty boring.

00:23:49.590 --> 00:23:51.420
The thing is, we actually don't think

00:23:51.420 --> 00:23:53.040
this clause of the Constitution

00:23:53.040 --> 00:23:55.170
is as essential to the gun debate

00:23:55.170 --> 00:23:56.460
as it's been made out to be.

00:23:56.460 --> 00:23:58.440
Because let's say you do take

00:23:58.440 --> 00:24:01.740
the strongest individualist
interpretation of the amendment,

00:24:01.740 --> 00:24:03.690
holding that the Founding Fathers

00:24:03.690 --> 00:24:06.966
believed in the protected
individual right to own a gun.

00:24:06.966 --> 00:24:09.540
Well, the Founding Fathers, as you know,

00:24:09.540 --> 00:24:12.240
also believed in lots of gun control,

00:24:12.240 --> 00:24:16.260
so clearly they felt that this
right could be infringed on

00:24:16.260 --> 00:24:18.060
if it meant protecting public safety.

00:24:18.060 --> 00:24:20.400
That is to say, you can both believe

00:24:20.400 --> 00:24:22.890
in the individualist
interpretation of the amendment

00:24:22.890 --> 00:24:26.790
and believe that gun control
measures are necessary.

00:24:26.790 --> 00:24:30.660
Because you, yes you, contain multitudes.

00:24:30.660 --> 00:24:32.850
But back to the '70s.

00:24:32.850 --> 00:24:36.000
Now armed with revelatory
constitutional backup,

00:24:36.000 --> 00:24:38.280
the NRA leapt into the decade,

00:24:38.280 --> 00:24:40.710
establishing an official lobbying wing,

00:24:40.710 --> 00:24:43.260
where both the NRA and cocaine,

00:24:43.260 --> 00:24:45.240
the '70s were a hell of a decade.

00:24:45.240 --> 00:24:47.430
Ironically, though mostly white,

00:24:47.430 --> 00:24:50.100
the group borrowed a lot of
the Black Panthers' arguments

00:24:50.100 --> 00:24:52.590
that guns were necessary for self-defense,

00:24:52.590 --> 00:24:55.230
not only in the home but on the street,

00:24:55.230 --> 00:24:58.410
where bumbling cops surely
couldn't be relied upon.

00:24:58.410 --> 00:24:59.880
There was some lingering tension, though,

00:24:59.880 --> 00:25:01.290
between the old guards

00:25:01.290 --> 00:25:03.191
who had cooperated on gun control laws,

00:25:03.191 --> 00:25:05.580
and newer, more radical members

00:25:05.580 --> 00:25:07.800
who believed any form of gun control

00:25:07.800 --> 00:25:10.620
was a slippery slope to
having their gun room

00:25:10.620 --> 00:25:12.630
raided by communists.

00:25:12.630 --> 00:25:15.270
The anti-gun control
extremists staged a coup

00:25:15.270 --> 00:25:17.150
at the NRA's national meeting in '77,

00:25:17.150 --> 00:25:20.910
setting the NRA on a more aggressive path.

00:25:20.910 --> 00:25:21.743
At the same time,

00:25:21.743 --> 00:25:23.460
a decision by the Supreme Court

00:25:23.460 --> 00:25:25.740
allowed for unfettered political spending.

00:25:25.740 --> 00:25:29.220
The NRA refocused on campaign
donations and fundraising,

00:25:29.220 --> 00:25:33.360
funneling millions into local,
state and national elections.

00:25:33.360 --> 00:25:36.270
In the process, they gained
some serious lobbying power.

00:25:36.270 --> 00:25:38.670
Not that much gun control
was liable to happen

00:25:38.670 --> 00:25:40.200
under the winner of the NRA's

00:25:40.200 --> 00:25:43.320
first presidential
endorsement, Ronald Reagan.

00:25:43.320 --> 00:25:44.940
Energized by Reagan's win,

00:25:44.940 --> 00:25:48.360
the NRA pushed legislation that
protected the gun industry.

00:25:48.360 --> 00:25:51.450
That included the Firearm
Owners Protection Act,

00:25:51.450 --> 00:25:53.730
which restricted
inspections of gun dealers,

00:25:53.730 --> 00:25:55.950
loosened the rules on
interstate gun sales,

00:25:55.950 --> 00:25:59.640
and made a federal database
of gun ownership illegal.

00:25:59.640 --> 00:26:00.930
Speaking off the record,

00:26:00.930 --> 00:26:03.990
some politicians admitted that
they only voted for the law

00:26:03.990 --> 00:26:06.960
because they were intimidated
by the NRA's power.

00:26:06.960 --> 00:26:09.240
What a system of government we have.

00:26:09.240 --> 00:26:11.910
Other big wins included
making it extremely difficult

00:26:11.910 --> 00:26:13.230
to sue gun manufacturers,

00:26:13.230 --> 00:26:15.270
removing law enforcement's
power to use discretion

00:26:15.270 --> 00:26:17.250
when issuing concealed carry licenses,

00:26:17.250 --> 00:26:20.160
and preventing local governments
from regulating firearms.

00:26:20.160 --> 00:26:24.180
But as the '80s wore on, NRA
membership started declining.

00:26:24.180 --> 00:26:26.370
Once again, it would take the threat

00:26:26.370 --> 00:26:28.050
of new federal gun control

00:26:28.050 --> 00:26:30.270
to bring the organization back to life.

00:26:30.270 --> 00:26:32.730
Jim Brady was serving as
White House Press Secretary

00:26:32.730 --> 00:26:34.940
when he was shot, and partially paralyzed,

00:26:34.940 --> 00:26:38.640
during an assassination
attempt on Reagan in 1981.

00:26:38.640 --> 00:26:41.300
His attacker, coincidentally,
was just released from prison

00:26:41.300 --> 00:26:43.740
and now has a sizable
social media following

00:26:43.740 --> 00:26:46.230
because we live in a weird world.

00:26:46.230 --> 00:26:48.330
Brady and his wife became
gun control activists,

00:26:48.330 --> 00:26:50.910
and in 1988, they
introduced the Brady Bill,

00:26:50.910 --> 00:26:53.250
requiring background
checks on all handguns

00:26:53.250 --> 00:26:54.630
sold by licensed dealers.

00:26:54.630 --> 00:26:56.760
It finally passed in 1993,

00:26:56.760 --> 00:27:00.210
followed by an assault
weapons ban in 1994,

00:27:00.210 --> 00:27:03.150
both of which inflamed the NRA.

00:27:03.150 --> 00:27:05.527
As the Washington Post
explains in its book,

00:27:05.527 --> 00:27:06.600
"Guns in America,"

00:27:06.600 --> 00:27:09.900
NRA rhetoric got gnarlier and gnarlier.

00:27:09.900 --> 00:27:12.780
In a fundraising letter,
President Wayne LaPierre wrote

00:27:12.780 --> 00:27:14.227
that the assault weapons ban,

00:27:14.227 --> 00:27:16.807
"Gives jackbooted government thugs

00:27:16.807 --> 00:27:19.477
"more power to take away
our constitutional rights,

00:27:19.477 --> 00:27:20.647
"break in our doors,

00:27:20.647 --> 00:27:21.577
"seize our guns,

00:27:21.577 --> 00:27:25.110
"destroy our property and
even injure and kill us."

00:27:25.110 --> 00:27:27.300
When one-time NRA member Timothy McVeigh

00:27:27.300 --> 00:27:29.490
blew up the Alfred P.
Murrah Federal Building

00:27:29.490 --> 00:27:31.950
in Oklahoma City in April of 1995,

00:27:31.950 --> 00:27:35.220
the NRA was criticized for
its anti-government rhetoric,

00:27:35.220 --> 00:27:37.950
which some thought
encouraged the militant.

00:27:37.950 --> 00:27:39.750
Why ever would they think that?

00:27:39.750 --> 00:27:41.790
The organization tried
to improve its image

00:27:41.790 --> 00:27:44.490
under the presidency of
famous actor Charleton Heston

00:27:44.490 --> 00:27:45.900
through great PR move

00:27:45.900 --> 00:27:48.480
like holding its national
convention in Denver

00:27:48.480 --> 00:27:50.400
two weeks after Columbine.

00:27:50.400 --> 00:27:52.590
Surely a mistake they'd never make again.

00:27:52.590 --> 00:27:55.110
Except for when they did the
exact same thing in Houston

00:27:55.110 --> 00:27:57.540
three days after the Uvalde shooting.

00:27:57.540 --> 00:28:00.660
Great job, NRA, really good planning.

00:28:00.660 --> 00:28:02.760
More strategically, the
group allied heavily

00:28:02.760 --> 00:28:05.280
with George W. Bush's campaign in 2000,

00:28:05.280 --> 00:28:07.830
leading to this delicious bit of PR.

00:28:07.830 --> 00:28:10.680
- For everyone within
the sound of my voice

00:28:10.680 --> 00:28:13.140
to hear and to heed,

00:28:13.140 --> 00:28:18.140
and especially for you, Mr. Gore.

00:28:18.732 --> 00:28:19.860
(audience laughing)

00:28:19.860 --> 00:28:23.026
From my cold dead hands!

00:28:23.026 --> 00:28:25.560
- Citing Bush's eventual victory,

00:28:25.560 --> 00:28:27.360
Fortune magazine ranked the NRA

00:28:27.360 --> 00:28:30.720
the most powerful lobbying
organization in the country.

00:28:30.720 --> 00:28:33.810
But the aughts brought
victory after victory.

00:28:33.810 --> 00:28:35.987
The assault rifle ban expired in 2004,

00:28:35.987 --> 00:28:38.640
and in 2008 the Supreme Court knocked down

00:28:38.640 --> 00:28:40.020
the D.C. handgun ban.

00:28:40.020 --> 00:28:42.660
Ironically, the NRA may
have lobbied its way

00:28:42.660 --> 00:28:43.800
out of relevancy.

00:28:43.800 --> 00:28:45.630
The organization is strongest

00:28:45.630 --> 00:28:48.480
when people feel their gun
rights are under attack.

00:28:48.480 --> 00:28:50.880
The organization would
eventually lose steam

00:28:50.880 --> 00:28:53.190
in the late 2010s, thanks to lawsuits,

00:28:53.190 --> 00:28:55.920
leadership scandals, a bankruptcy
filing that was rejected,

00:28:55.920 --> 00:28:57.660
and overall declining membership.

00:28:57.660 --> 00:28:58.950
Their political donations

00:28:58.950 --> 00:29:00.641
have proportionately dwindled down,

00:29:00.641 --> 00:29:03.120
although they still managed to spend

00:29:03.120 --> 00:29:06.960
over $29 million dollars in
the 2020 federal elections.

00:29:06.960 --> 00:29:10.254
With the NRA dying a slow
death, it's worth asking,

00:29:10.254 --> 00:29:13.350
what does that mean for gun culture?

00:29:13.350 --> 00:29:15.810
Honestly, not a whole lot.

00:29:15.810 --> 00:29:17.970
Gun rights continues to be a winning issue

00:29:17.970 --> 00:29:20.460
for Republicans courting primary voters.

00:29:20.460 --> 00:29:23.880
Lawmakers might no longer
fear retaliation from the NRA,

00:29:23.880 --> 00:29:25.440
but they do fear looking like

00:29:25.440 --> 00:29:27.720
they're trying to take away people's guns.

00:29:27.720 --> 00:29:30.930
Meanwhile, gun sales have
nearly tripled since 2000,

00:29:30.930 --> 00:29:33.960
spiking especially over
the past three years.

00:29:33.960 --> 00:29:36.990
Presumably, so people could
shoot the novel coronavirus

00:29:36.990 --> 00:29:37.860
and not get sick.

00:29:37.860 --> 00:29:39.720
We live in violent times,

00:29:39.720 --> 00:29:43.320
with American gun deaths up 34% from 2019.

00:29:43.320 --> 00:29:47.010
And that's a 49% increase
over the previous five years.

00:29:47.010 --> 00:29:48.180
In the last 10 years,

00:29:48.180 --> 00:29:50.550
that figure's risen 75%.

00:29:50.550 --> 00:29:53.310
With faith in law
enforcement generally down,

00:29:53.310 --> 00:29:55.800
it's no wonder that people
are looking to arm themselves,

00:29:55.800 --> 00:29:57.480
or increase their stockpile.

00:29:57.480 --> 00:30:00.480
And that idea brings us back
to the issue of self-defense,

00:30:00.480 --> 00:30:03.000
which has been sold as the
central reason to buy a gun

00:30:03.000 --> 00:30:04.680
since the mid-19th century,

00:30:04.680 --> 00:30:06.450
and has only become a
more compelling reason

00:30:06.450 --> 00:30:07.580
in recent years.

00:30:07.580 --> 00:30:10.200
(machine gun firing)
(woman screaming)

00:30:10.200 --> 00:30:11.490
- [Woman] Hunny, can
you bring mommy the mop?

00:30:11.490 --> 00:30:13.500
- Now, wanting a gun to protect yourself

00:30:13.500 --> 00:30:15.930
in a country that's
swimming in them is fair.

00:30:15.930 --> 00:30:18.420
But if you think owning a gun makes you,

00:30:18.420 --> 00:30:20.460
pardon the expression, bulletproof,

00:30:20.460 --> 00:30:22.650
the data really doesn't bear that out.

00:30:22.650 --> 00:30:25.557
Estimates of the number of
defensive gun uses range from

00:30:25.557 --> 00:30:28.080
100,000 to over 2 million per year,

00:30:28.080 --> 00:30:30.750
depending on methodologies, sample size,

00:30:30.750 --> 00:30:32.910
and the definition of defensive gun use.

00:30:32.910 --> 00:30:34.380
Given discrepancies in data,

00:30:34.380 --> 00:30:36.570
criminologist Thomas Gabor recommends

00:30:36.570 --> 00:30:39.060
looking at police officers
as a reference point,

00:30:39.060 --> 00:30:41.730
since they are armed,
trained to wield guns,

00:30:41.730 --> 00:30:44.040
and not infrequently encounter danger.

00:30:44.040 --> 00:30:47.010
According to James Fyfe, an
expert on police gun use,

00:30:47.010 --> 00:30:49.080
a big-city cop is involved in a shootout

00:30:49.080 --> 00:30:52.590
every 450 years, on average.

00:30:52.590 --> 00:30:57.590
Again, that's every 450 years, on average.

00:30:58.020 --> 00:31:00.990
Almost half of a thousand years.

00:31:00.990 --> 00:31:03.570
Another calculation found
that in any given year,

00:31:03.570 --> 00:31:07.410
only one in 170 cops is
involved in a shooting.

00:31:07.410 --> 00:31:08.767
As Gabor concludes,

00:31:08.767 --> 00:31:11.047
"These numbers show that
even police officers,

00:31:11.047 --> 00:31:12.967
"who are armed and are
more routinely involved

00:31:12.967 --> 00:31:15.037
"in dangerous situations
than the average citizen,

00:31:15.037 --> 00:31:17.850
"rarely rely on guns
when doing police work."

00:31:17.850 --> 00:31:20.970
Unless their talking to a
harmless, young Black person,

00:31:20.970 --> 00:31:22.530
then they definitely need guns.

00:31:22.530 --> 00:31:24.660
No, that's not gonna go in
the video, but it's true.

00:31:24.660 --> 00:31:25.710
This raises the question,

00:31:25.710 --> 00:31:28.590
why would people doing
far less dangerous jobs

00:31:28.590 --> 00:31:30.420
need to be armed all the time?

00:31:30.420 --> 00:31:31.830
But there's a much deeper problem

00:31:31.830 --> 00:31:33.090
with the self-defense argument.

00:31:33.090 --> 00:31:35.790
Research from the Centers of
Disease Control and Prevention

00:31:35.790 --> 00:31:38.160
found that more than
three-quarters of homicide victims

00:31:38.160 --> 00:31:39.330
knew their attacker.

00:31:39.330 --> 00:31:41.799
Because of this, bringing
a gun into the home

00:31:41.799 --> 00:31:44.850
actually poses more of a
threat to the residents

00:31:44.850 --> 00:31:47.100
than it poses defense against intruders.

00:31:47.100 --> 00:31:49.693
Sort of like, if you bring
36 beers into your house,

00:31:49.693 --> 00:31:52.620
you're more likely to
get drunk from the beers

00:31:52.620 --> 00:31:54.900
than if you didn't bring
the beers into your house.

00:31:54.900 --> 00:31:56.700
Was that a scientific comparison? No.

00:31:56.700 --> 00:31:58.860
But if you bring 36 beers into your house,

00:31:58.860 --> 00:31:59.910
what do you thinks gonna happen?

00:31:59.910 --> 00:32:02.190
This is particularly true for women,

00:32:02.190 --> 00:32:04.380
who are two to four times more likely

00:32:04.380 --> 00:32:06.000
to be intentionally killed by a gun

00:32:06.000 --> 00:32:07.920
if they live in a house with one.

00:32:07.920 --> 00:32:09.810
If the person you're most afraid of

00:32:09.810 --> 00:32:11.700
is the person sharing your bed,

00:32:11.700 --> 00:32:12.954
putting a gun under your pillow

00:32:12.954 --> 00:32:15.780
isn't exactly a setup for safety.

00:32:15.780 --> 00:32:16.980
As Harvard professor

00:32:16.980 --> 00:32:19.777
and public health expert
David Hemenway summarizes,

00:32:19.777 --> 00:32:21.187
"Scientific studies suggest

00:32:21.187 --> 00:32:23.347
"that the health risk of a gun in the home

00:32:23.347 --> 00:32:24.720
"is greater than the benefit,"

00:32:24.720 --> 00:32:26.437
and that, "a gun in the home

00:32:26.437 --> 00:32:29.557
"may more likely be used
to threaten intimates

00:32:29.557 --> 00:32:31.680
"than to protect against intruders."

00:32:31.680 --> 00:32:33.930
Add to that the increased
likelihood of homicide,

00:32:33.930 --> 00:32:36.990
suicide and accidental deaths
occurring in houses with guns,

00:32:36.990 --> 00:32:40.050
and it becomes an even
more dangerous prospect.

00:32:40.050 --> 00:32:42.030
By the way, we've linked
two domestic abuse

00:32:42.030 --> 00:32:43.920
and suicide support
lines in the description.

00:32:43.920 --> 00:32:45.960
This all suggests that, on a micro level,

00:32:45.960 --> 00:32:48.750
the mere presence of guns
increases the likelihood

00:32:48.750 --> 00:32:50.670
of multiple forms of violence.

00:32:50.670 --> 00:32:52.410
This has been borne
out in other countries.

00:32:52.410 --> 00:32:54.750
For example, when Israel
stopped letting soldiers

00:32:54.750 --> 00:32:56.520
bring their guns home on weekends,

00:32:56.520 --> 00:33:00.150
soldier suicides decreased
by a staggering 40%.

00:33:00.150 --> 00:33:02.070
This all suggests that having guns around

00:33:02.070 --> 00:33:04.380
simply makes violence more likely.

00:33:04.380 --> 00:33:06.030
Again, if you're in a bar,

00:33:06.030 --> 00:33:07.410
you're more likely to get drunk.

00:33:07.410 --> 00:33:08.340
If you're in a bathroom,

00:33:08.340 --> 00:33:10.200
you're more likely to...

00:33:10.200 --> 00:33:11.970
go to the bathroom.

00:33:11.970 --> 00:33:13.950
The failure of guns to deter crime

00:33:13.950 --> 00:33:16.290
are clear on a regional level as well.

00:33:16.290 --> 00:33:18.960
In 2014, the South was the
region with the highest rate

00:33:18.960 --> 00:33:20.550
of violent and property crimes;

00:33:20.550 --> 00:33:22.230
the Northeast had the lowest.

00:33:22.230 --> 00:33:24.840
42% of households in the South had a gun,

00:33:24.840 --> 00:33:27.540
compared to 25% of
houses in the Northeast.

00:33:27.540 --> 00:33:30.157
As Gabor concludes, "These
regional comparisons

00:33:30.157 --> 00:33:32.107
"provide no support for the idea

00:33:32.107 --> 00:33:33.607
"that higher gun ownership levels

00:33:33.607 --> 00:33:35.820
"serve as a net deterrent to crime."

00:33:35.820 --> 00:33:37.980
Now, there's an obvious reason gun sales

00:33:37.980 --> 00:33:39.840
tend to go up after a mass shooting.

00:33:39.840 --> 00:33:41.730
People want to be prepared.

00:33:41.730 --> 00:33:44.280
Although they account for
approximately one-half

00:33:44.280 --> 00:33:46.530
of 1% of gun violence in the country,

00:33:46.530 --> 00:33:48.870
mass shootings garner the most headlines

00:33:48.870 --> 00:33:50.130
and political attention.

00:33:50.130 --> 00:33:51.390
And this makes sense.

00:33:51.390 --> 00:33:53.190
Though we're more likely to be killed

00:33:53.190 --> 00:33:55.830
by a family member or a
friend than by a stranger,

00:33:55.830 --> 00:33:57.060
mass shootings remind us

00:33:57.060 --> 00:33:59.280
that we can't simply avoid gun violence

00:33:59.280 --> 00:34:03.120
just by being nice to our mom,
or picking the right friends.

00:34:03.120 --> 00:34:04.770
The first modern mass shooting

00:34:04.770 --> 00:34:06.840
as defined by journalist Seamus McGraw,

00:34:06.840 --> 00:34:10.530
who I would bet my life is
a Catholic occurred in 1966,

00:34:10.530 --> 00:34:13.140
when a man opened fire
from a tower in Texas

00:34:13.140 --> 00:34:14.910
and killed 17 people.

00:34:14.910 --> 00:34:18.900
And so, the romanticized image
of the lone American gunman

00:34:18.900 --> 00:34:20.550
protecting himself from bears

00:34:20.550 --> 00:34:23.190
mutated into the lone
gunman turning his weapon

00:34:23.190 --> 00:34:26.760
on innocent civilians with
no clear motive or agenda.

00:34:26.760 --> 00:34:28.560
Over the coming decades, the mass shooter,

00:34:28.560 --> 00:34:31.200
typically young, white, and male,

00:34:31.200 --> 00:34:34.110
would become a collective
boogeyman in the American psyche.

00:34:34.110 --> 00:34:35.760
- [Reporter] Tonight
authorities say they've arrested

00:34:35.760 --> 00:34:36.870
the alleged gunman,

00:34:36.870 --> 00:34:39.240
identified as an 18-year-old white male

00:34:39.240 --> 00:34:40.530
from Conklin, New York.

00:34:40.530 --> 00:34:42.330
- In response to recent mass shootings,

00:34:42.330 --> 00:34:44.310
including the one at Uvalde Elementary,

00:34:44.310 --> 00:34:46.560
there have been calls to arm teachers.

00:34:46.560 --> 00:34:48.240
We should be skeptical of the idea

00:34:48.240 --> 00:34:51.480
that a good guy with a gun
could offer much protection

00:34:51.480 --> 00:34:52.830
in the event of a mass shooting.

00:34:52.830 --> 00:34:55.410
First of all, not even
cops, who are trained,

00:34:55.410 --> 00:34:57.540
hit their target the majority of the time.

00:34:57.540 --> 00:35:00.510
Indeed, inspector Kevin
Maloney of the NYPD

00:35:00.510 --> 00:35:01.920
reported that in 2017,

00:35:01.920 --> 00:35:05.640
city cops hit their target
just 44% of the time.

00:35:05.640 --> 00:35:08.940
Also, if there was a
platonic name for NYPD cop

00:35:08.940 --> 00:35:10.770
it would be Kevin Maloney.

00:35:10.770 --> 00:35:13.020
Logically, you would
have to expect a teacher

00:35:13.020 --> 00:35:14.670
to underperform a cop.

00:35:14.670 --> 00:35:16.170
And, in a crowded public space,

00:35:16.170 --> 00:35:19.140
missing your mark can
mean killing someone else.

00:35:19.140 --> 00:35:21.930
Secondly, having multiple
people firing guns

00:35:21.930 --> 00:35:25.980
at a crime scene is a recipe
for police confusion and chaos.

00:35:25.980 --> 00:35:29.100
Police learn this at
the Texas Tower in 1966,

00:35:29.100 --> 00:35:32.310
when several onlookers started
firing back at the shooter,

00:35:32.310 --> 00:35:34.200
stoking even more panic.

00:35:34.200 --> 00:35:36.217
Journalist Seamus McGraw
writes in his book

00:35:36.217 --> 00:35:37.537
"From a Taller Tower," that,

00:35:37.537 --> 00:35:39.847
"As John McDonald, executive
director of security

00:35:39.847 --> 00:35:42.367
"at Jefferson County Public
Schools told the Denver Post,

00:35:42.367 --> 00:35:45.457
"in such situations, adding
another gun to the mix

00:35:45.457 --> 00:35:47.310
"can have tragic consequences."

00:35:47.310 --> 00:35:50.400
Thirdly, modern firearms
have become so lethal

00:35:50.400 --> 00:35:54.030
that a bystander can't really
prevent profound loss of life.

00:35:54.030 --> 00:35:55.440
In one example in Ohio,

00:35:55.440 --> 00:35:57.450
the police responded within seconds,

00:35:57.450 --> 00:35:59.460
but not quickly enough to stop the shooter

00:35:59.460 --> 00:36:02.777
from killing nine people
and wounding 27 more.

00:36:02.777 --> 00:36:05.580
Even if Jason Bourne was your teacher,

00:36:05.580 --> 00:36:08.190
even he likely couldn't respond in time.

00:36:08.190 --> 00:36:09.060
Given these facts,

00:36:09.060 --> 00:36:13.590
the results of FBI studies of
277 active-shooter scenarios

00:36:13.590 --> 00:36:14.550
make sense.

00:36:14.550 --> 00:36:18.030
They found that only 3.9% concluded

00:36:18.030 --> 00:36:21.180
with a good guy with a gun firing a shot.

00:36:21.180 --> 00:36:25.440
Meanwhile, 11% were stopped
by unarmed civilians.

00:36:25.440 --> 00:36:29.874
Again, that means, that unarmed
civilians have done more

00:36:29.874 --> 00:36:33.270
to stop mass shootings
than good guys with guns.

00:36:33.270 --> 00:36:34.950
What's more, if we want
to know what happens

00:36:34.950 --> 00:36:37.650
when you have more good guys
with guns in their pockets,

00:36:37.650 --> 00:36:40.290
we can simply look to some
Latin American countries

00:36:40.290 --> 00:36:42.180
where guns are basically ubiquitous,

00:36:42.180 --> 00:36:45.060
yet certainly have not
made communities safer.

00:36:45.060 --> 00:36:47.550
Rather, well-armed
countries like Guatemala,

00:36:47.550 --> 00:36:49.980
Honduras, and El Savador
have some of the world's

00:36:49.980 --> 00:36:51.720
highest homicide rates.

00:36:51.720 --> 00:36:52.830
As Rebecca Peters,

00:36:52.830 --> 00:36:54.990
former director of the
International Action Network

00:36:54.990 --> 00:36:56.377
on Small Arms, argues,

00:36:56.377 --> 00:36:59.017
"A society that is
relying on guys with guns

00:36:59.017 --> 00:37:01.087
"to stop violence is a sign of a society

00:37:01.087 --> 00:37:02.767
"where institutions have broken down.

00:37:02.767 --> 00:37:05.227
"It's shocking to hear
anyone in the United States

00:37:05.227 --> 00:37:06.367
"considering a solution

00:37:06.367 --> 00:37:08.490
"that would make it seem
more like Colombia."

00:37:08.490 --> 00:37:09.323
Also worth noting,

00:37:09.323 --> 00:37:10.890
many of those countries have guns

00:37:10.890 --> 00:37:14.070
because Reagan shipped them
down there in the '80s.

00:37:14.070 --> 00:37:15.030
You just Google that.

00:37:15.030 --> 00:37:16.560
It's all real. I swear to God.

00:37:16.560 --> 00:37:18.150
In contrast though, Switzerland

00:37:18.150 --> 00:37:19.830
has a high rate of gun ownership,

00:37:19.830 --> 00:37:22.440
but a very low rate of gun homicides,

00:37:22.440 --> 00:37:24.180
in part because guns are mostly seen

00:37:24.180 --> 00:37:25.890
as official military weapons,

00:37:25.890 --> 00:37:27.960
rather than tools for self-defense.

00:37:27.960 --> 00:37:30.180
Switzerland brings us
to a separate question.

00:37:30.180 --> 00:37:33.240
Why is America so uniquely violent

00:37:33.240 --> 00:37:35.070
compared to other wealthy nations?

00:37:35.070 --> 00:37:36.330
There's a lot of factors,

00:37:36.330 --> 00:37:38.280
but you probably won't be surprised

00:37:38.280 --> 00:37:40.706
that other countries are
worried that American violence

00:37:40.706 --> 00:37:42.361
might be contagious.

00:37:42.361 --> 00:37:44.760
As then Prime Minister John Howard said

00:37:44.760 --> 00:37:47.917
after Australia's Port
Arthur Massacre in 1996,

00:37:47.917 --> 00:37:50.317
"We do not want the American disease

00:37:50.317 --> 00:37:51.960
"imported into Australia."

00:37:51.960 --> 00:37:55.440
Unlike in America, mass
shootings in comparable countries

00:37:55.440 --> 00:37:58.020
have been followed by
sweeping gun control action.

00:37:58.020 --> 00:38:01.050
And while it's almost
impossible to imagine America

00:38:01.050 --> 00:38:03.690
taking action to meaningfully
curb gun violence,

00:38:03.690 --> 00:38:05.970
at least with all the
partisan gridlock in play,

00:38:05.970 --> 00:38:08.340
many other countries have pulled it off.

00:38:08.340 --> 00:38:10.110
After the previously mentioned massacre,

00:38:10.110 --> 00:38:11.700
Australia swiftly rolled out

00:38:11.700 --> 00:38:14.100
stringent gun ownership
requirements and restrictions,

00:38:14.100 --> 00:38:16.620
along with a huge gun buyback program.

00:38:16.620 --> 00:38:19.800
After a school shooting
in the UK, also in 1996,

00:38:19.800 --> 00:38:22.830
Parliament rolled out a
similarly strict set of laws,

00:38:22.830 --> 00:38:26.370
and there hasn't been a school
shooting in the UK since.

00:38:26.370 --> 00:38:29.100
In other words, The Onion got us again.

00:38:29.100 --> 00:38:30.780
Which makes it all the more depressing

00:38:30.780 --> 00:38:34.110
that American gun violence
continues unabated.

00:38:34.110 --> 00:38:37.542
73% of mass shootings in
industrialized countries

00:38:37.542 --> 00:38:40.650
have occurred in ye olde United States,

00:38:40.650 --> 00:38:44.370
even though we make up
just 5% of the population.

00:38:44.370 --> 00:38:45.622
As of early June,

00:38:45.622 --> 00:38:49.362
there had been more
than 250 mass shootings

00:38:49.362 --> 00:38:52.803
in the U.S. this year alone.

00:38:53.700 --> 00:38:57.390
Where does this uniquely
American violence come from?

00:38:57.390 --> 00:38:58.650
According to McGraw,

00:38:58.650 --> 00:39:00.720
some of it is the result
of a media culture

00:39:00.720 --> 00:39:02.580
that valorizes fame.

00:39:02.580 --> 00:39:06.097
He writes, "That thirst for
fame runs deep in our culture,

00:39:06.097 --> 00:39:08.580
"and it can make people
do terrible things."

00:39:08.580 --> 00:39:12.240
Now obviously America is very
much a fame-obsessed society,

00:39:12.240 --> 00:39:14.250
but there are plenty
of aspiring celebrities

00:39:14.250 --> 00:39:16.830
all over the world and you
don't hear about Scottish goths

00:39:16.830 --> 00:39:18.420
shooting up schools for the clout.

00:39:18.420 --> 00:39:21.840
So that still doesn't explain
what makes mass shootings

00:39:21.840 --> 00:39:24.030
so damn American.

00:39:24.030 --> 00:39:27.240
Some of it's rooted in good
old-fashioned prejudice.

00:39:27.240 --> 00:39:29.760
Frequently, mass shooters
are taking their anger out

00:39:29.760 --> 00:39:33.390
on an identity group, whether
another race, as in Buffalo;

00:39:33.390 --> 00:39:35.610
religion, as in the
Tree of Life synagogue;

00:39:35.610 --> 00:39:38.310
or women, as in Isla Vista, California.

00:39:38.310 --> 00:39:40.890
But in recent years, there's
been a chilling development

00:39:40.890 --> 00:39:43.380
of mass shooters streaming their violence,

00:39:43.380 --> 00:39:46.770
and going viral in the dark
crevices of the internet.

00:39:46.770 --> 00:39:48.300
This strengthens the idea

00:39:48.300 --> 00:39:51.900
that they're searching for
some sort of twisted glory.

00:39:51.900 --> 00:39:55.440
Maybe in their heads they
are the lone American gunman

00:39:55.440 --> 00:39:57.510
romanticized throughout our culture.

00:39:57.510 --> 00:39:59.970
Indeed, McGraw notes that
certain types of guns

00:39:59.970 --> 00:40:03.150
take on a fetishistic element
amongst mass shooters;

00:40:03.150 --> 00:40:07.110
a sort of twisted extension
of our national love of guns.

00:40:07.110 --> 00:40:09.990
When a mass shooting happens,
there's an instant rush

00:40:09.990 --> 00:40:13.650
to figure out the cause of
such senseless violence.

00:40:13.650 --> 00:40:15.510
And there's inevitably talk

00:40:15.510 --> 00:40:17.670
about the mental illness
crisis in America,

00:40:17.670 --> 00:40:20.730
which, while being a very real problem,

00:40:20.730 --> 00:40:22.200
is a bit of a misdirection.

00:40:22.200 --> 00:40:25.650
Stats suggest that up
to 61% of mass shooters

00:40:25.650 --> 00:40:27.600
have some form of mental illness.

00:40:27.600 --> 00:40:29.970
And certainly, if America
had better resources

00:40:29.970 --> 00:40:31.110
for mental health care,

00:40:31.110 --> 00:40:32.910
maybe those shooters
could have been treated

00:40:32.910 --> 00:40:34.689
before resorting to violence.

00:40:34.689 --> 00:40:37.260
But what about the other 40%?

00:40:37.260 --> 00:40:39.630
While certainly not in a great headspace,

00:40:39.630 --> 00:40:41.730
given the whole mass shooting thing,

00:40:41.730 --> 00:40:44.550
these people aren't
explicitly mentally ill.

00:40:44.550 --> 00:40:47.100
Rather, they often exhibit
signs of narcissism,

00:40:47.100 --> 00:40:49.950
with a deep sense of
grievance against the world.

00:40:49.950 --> 00:40:52.530
But we can't just go
arresting all the narcissists

00:40:52.530 --> 00:40:56.160
on the off chance that
they can go postal, can we?

00:40:56.160 --> 00:40:57.450
Wait, can we?

00:40:57.450 --> 00:40:59.610
'Cause I have a list of
some narcissists in my life

00:40:59.610 --> 00:41:01.170
that I would love to give the authorities.

00:41:01.170 --> 00:41:04.710
What's more, some 80% of
guns used in mass shootings

00:41:04.710 --> 00:41:06.840
were purchased legally,

00:41:06.840 --> 00:41:09.990
so it's not like better
enforcement of existing laws

00:41:09.990 --> 00:41:11.280
could have stopped them.

00:41:11.280 --> 00:41:12.450
There is one thing

00:41:12.450 --> 00:41:14.580
most mass shooters have in common, though,

00:41:14.580 --> 00:41:15.930
domestic violence.

00:41:15.930 --> 00:41:17.790
Over two-thirds of mass shooters

00:41:17.790 --> 00:41:19.860
either killed family or intimate partners,

00:41:19.860 --> 00:41:22.590
or were involved in
domestic violence disputes.

00:41:22.590 --> 00:41:23.700
On a larger scale,

00:41:23.700 --> 00:41:26.280
the overlap of domestic
violence and mass shootings

00:41:26.280 --> 00:41:29.100
suggests that, rather than
being some kind of fluke

00:41:29.100 --> 00:41:32.610
or outlier, they are an extension
of the broader structures

00:41:32.610 --> 00:41:35.040
of everyday American violence.

00:41:35.040 --> 00:41:36.480
Much of which is predicated

00:41:36.480 --> 00:41:39.870
on the extreme lethality of modern guns.

00:41:39.870 --> 00:41:42.690
So is there something embedded in the DNA

00:41:42.690 --> 00:41:45.150
of America's history that just makes us

00:41:45.150 --> 00:41:46.740
more prone to shoot people?

00:41:46.740 --> 00:41:47.640
We're not sure.

00:41:47.640 --> 00:41:49.860
But it does seem like a series of factors

00:41:49.860 --> 00:41:52.530
ranging from America's colonial past,

00:41:52.530 --> 00:41:54.030
to western expansion,

00:41:54.030 --> 00:41:55.710
to an economy built on war,

00:41:55.710 --> 00:41:58.290
to an emphasis on individual
liberty above all else,

00:41:58.290 --> 00:42:00.030
to underfunded mental health care,

00:42:00.030 --> 00:42:01.770
to the way domestic violence is ignored

00:42:01.770 --> 00:42:02.880
until it becomes lethal.

00:42:02.880 --> 00:42:04.260
Yeah, that's a lot of stuff!

00:42:04.260 --> 00:42:06.510
There's a lot of things.

00:42:06.510 --> 00:42:08.250
Oh, it's very overwhelming.

00:42:08.250 --> 00:42:11.040
These things have all
coalesced in the modern era

00:42:11.040 --> 00:42:14.130
to make America #1 in mass shootings

00:42:14.130 --> 00:42:15.660
and other gun-related deaths.

00:42:15.660 --> 00:42:19.950
And because it's not just
one, or two, or three things

00:42:19.950 --> 00:42:21.180
at the root of this problem,

00:42:21.180 --> 00:42:24.330
it sadly seems like there's no easy fix.

00:42:24.330 --> 00:42:27.510
But hey, you can use an online dating app,

00:42:27.510 --> 00:42:29.400
meet and marry a Swiss national

00:42:29.400 --> 00:42:30.600
and then you can live in a country

00:42:30.600 --> 00:42:33.600
where you can own a gun and
eat unlimited Toblerones

00:42:33.600 --> 00:42:35.370
without worrying about mass shootings.

00:42:35.370 --> 00:42:36.960
But what do you guys think?

00:42:36.960 --> 00:42:40.208
Is there something inherently
violent about America?

00:42:40.208 --> 00:42:43.140
Can our gun violence
problem ever be fixed?

00:42:43.140 --> 00:42:45.480
If you know, please let
us know in the comments.

00:42:45.480 --> 00:42:47.790
And don't forget that
we've linked to resources

00:42:47.790 --> 00:42:49.650
for domestic abuse and suicide prevention

00:42:49.650 --> 00:42:50.640
in the description.

00:42:50.640 --> 00:42:53.340
We've also included a reading
list in the description

00:42:53.340 --> 00:42:55.230
if you're interested in learning more.

00:42:55.230 --> 00:42:57.360
Now smash that subscribe button

00:42:57.360 --> 00:42:59.400
like a Satanist destroying butter Jesus,

00:42:59.400 --> 00:43:01.380
and don't forget to ring that bell.

00:43:01.380 --> 00:43:03.870
A huge thanks to our patrons
for all your support.

00:43:03.870 --> 00:43:06.713
Be sure to check out our
revamped Patreon page.

00:43:06.713 --> 00:43:09.210
Remember to check out our
new stream Wisecrack Live

00:43:09.210 --> 00:43:11.880
that takes place every
Thursday right on this channel.

00:43:11.880 --> 00:43:14.851
And as always, thanks for watching. Later.

00:43:14.851 --> 00:43:17.518
